  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different glacier-fed lakes can evolve under climate warming and why this raises the danger of glacial lake outburst floods (GLOFs). Across the top and middle rows, three lake settings are compared in the same mountain valley: a supraglacial lake on the glacier surface, a proglacial lake at the glacier front, and an ice-dammed lake held back by ice. The upper row shows these lake types in the current climate, while the lower row shows their likely future state. In general, the lakes become larger or more developed as the glacier retreats and melt increases, suggesting growing water storage and instability.\n\nThe bottom panel links these changing lake conditions to increasing GLOF hazard potential. A mountain scene highlights a likely chain of impacts: a landslide or similar trigger reaches the lake, floodwater rushes downstream, and damage affects populated valley areas. Beside this, a simple risk diagram shows risk as the overlap of hazard, exposure, and vulnerability, with adaptation and countermeasures acting to reduce the danger. Overall, the figure conveys that warming climate can expand glacier-lake systems and amplify downstream flood risk.",
